            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "{\"strengths\":[\"Accurately explains why real GDP per capita improves cross-country comparability: it adjusts for inflation and population size.\",\"Develops relevant mechanisms through which higher output per person can support consumption and fund public services.\",\"Identifies several major limitations: inequality, unpaid work, environmental damage/resource depletion and defensive expenditure.\"],\"improvements\":[\"Use genuine real-world examples and connect each one to the limitation being evaluated—for example, contrast GDP per capita with income distribution or environmental outcomes in a named country.\",\"Turn the listed limitations into evaluation: explain when real GDP per capita remains a useful proxy for material living standards and when the omission is likely to be large.\",\"Replace the unsupported final conclusion with a clear judgement about its usefulness as a partial indicator, rather than saying simply that it is both useful and not useful.\"],\"mistakes\":[\"Underdeveloped evaluation\",\"No real-world example\",\"Unsupported judgement\"],\"examinerComment\":\"Estimated practice mark: the response shows sound knowledge and several relevant limitations, with a clear explanation of what real GDP per capita can indicate about material consumption and public-service capacity. However, the question explicitly requires real-world examples, and none are provided. Evaluation is present but mostly takes the form of a list; the conclusion does not weigh the conditions under which the measure is more or less useful. This fits the 7–9 band.\",\"studyNext\":\"Add one or two named country examples, then make a conditional judgement: real GDP per capita is most useful for comparing average material output/income, but less useful where inequality, non-market production or environmental costs are substantial.\",\"assessmentFormat\":\"paper_1_b\",\"paper\":\"paper_1\",\"questionPart\":\"b\",\"levelRelevance\":\"shared_sl_hl\",\"assessmentSkills\":[\"economic_analysis\",\"application\",\"evaluation\"],\"commandTerm\":\"evaluate\",\"commandTermLabel\":\"Evaluate\",\"syllabusUnit\":\"unit_4\",\"syllabusTopic\":\"4.8\",\"topicLabel\":\"Measuring development\",\"classificationConfidence\":\"high\",\"markingConfidence\":\"high\",\"diagramExpected\":false,\"diagramSubmitted\":false,\"diagramAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"workingsExpected\":false,\"workingsSubmitted\":false,\"workingsAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"attachmentContent\":\"none\",\"assessableEarned\":8,\"markBreakdown\":[{\"label\":\"Knowledge and terminology\",\"awarded\":3,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Accurate explanation of GDP, real GDP and the per-capita adjustment, although the focus could be tied more explicitly to living standards.\"},{\"label\":\"Economic analysis\",\"awarded\":3,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Explains consumption and tax-base channels and identifies relevant ways GDP per capita can misrepresent welfare; some points remain briefly asserted rather than fully developed.\"},{\"label\":\"Application to context\",\"awarded\":0,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"No named real-world country, event, policy or evidence is used despite the explicit requirement for real-world examples.\"},{\"label\":\"Evaluation and judgment\",\"awarded\":2,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"Recognises qualifications such as time horizon, distribution and environmental costs, but does not weigh them in a developed conditional judgement; the final conclusion is unsupported.\"},{\"label\":\"Structure and clarity\",\"awarded\":3,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The answer is concise and logically sequenced from usefulness to limitations, though the ending is generic.\"}],\"bandRationale\":\"Best fit: 7–9, middle. The response contains accurate, relevant theory and several valid limitations, with some evaluative awareness. Its lack of required real-world application and only partly developed weighing/judgement prevent the developed, supported evaluation expected in the 10–12 band.\",\"limitations\":[\"No real-world examples are supplied.\",\"The final judgement does not explain how the relative importance of the limitations affects the overall usefulness of real GDP per capita.\"],\"examinerJudgment\":{\"task\":\"Evaluate the usefulness of real GDP per capita as an indicator of living standards using real-world examples.\",\"demonstrated\":\"Accurate explanation of inflation and population adjustment, material-living-standard channels, and valid limitations involving distribution, unpaid work, environmental costs and defensive expenditure.\",\"materialLimitations\":[\"No real-world example is used.\",\"Evaluation is largely a list of caveats rather than a developed weighing of when the indicator is reliable.\",\"The conclusion is generic and not supported by the preceding analysis.\"],\"selectedBand\":\"7-9\",\"withinBand\":\"middle\",\"diagramEffect\":\"not_required\",\"summary\":\"Relevant theory and limited evaluation are securely demonstrated, but required application and a supported overall judgement are missing.\"},\"componentEvaluation\":null}"
          }
